    bio: 'Al-Ghazālī was born in Tus, in the Khorasan region of present-day Iran, in 1058. He rose with extraordinary speed through the intellectual world of the Abbasid caliphate, becoming the head of the Nizamiyya madrassa in Baghdad — the most prestigious academic institution in the Islamic world — at the age of thirty-three. His lectures drew enormous crowds, and his reputation as a theologian, jurist, and philosopher was matched by almost no one in the medieval world. He was, by every external measure, a figure of supreme success. And then, in 1095, he suffered a complete intellectual and psychological breakdown that would transform both his life and the subsequent history of Islamic thought.\n\nAl-Ghazālī describes the crisis in his extraordinary spiritual autobiography, "Deliverance from Error" (Munqidh min al-Dalal): a sudden, paralysing inability to be certain of anything — a sceptical collapse that left him speechless for two months and unable to continue his duties. After a long period of withdrawal, he abandoned his position in Baghdad and spent nearly a decade as a wandering Sufi mystic, travelling through Syria, Palestine, and Arabia, seeking the direct experiential knowledge that he had concluded rational philosophy could never provide. His great work, "The Incoherence of the Philosophers" (Tahafut al-Falasifa), was a systematic critique of the Aristotelian philosophical tradition as represented by al-Farabi and Ibn Sina, arguing that their metaphysical claims — on the eternity of the world, on divine knowledge, on bodily resurrection — were logically self-contradictory and religiously dangerous.\n\nHis masterpiece, "The Revival of the Religious Sciences" (Ihya Ulum al-Din), written in the years following his wandering, is one of the most comprehensive and influential works of Islamic thought ever produced — a forty-volume synthesis of jurisprudence, theology, ethics, and Sufi spirituality designed to revitalise a religious tradition he believed had become hollow and mechanical. Al-Ghazālī died in Tus in 1111, having returned to teaching in his final years. His impact on subsequent Islamic theology was so profound that he earned the title "Proof of Islam" (Hujjat al-Islam) — a rare honour reflecting the sense that his work had defended the tradition against its most sophisticated critics.',
    contributions: 'Al-Ghazālī pioneered a systematic critique of Aristotelian philosophy from within the Islamic tradition, argued that genuine religious knowledge must be grounded in direct experiential certainty rather than syllogistic reasoning, and developed an enormously influential synthesis of Sufi mystical practice with orthodox Islamic theology and jurisprudence. His philosophical scepticism — the demand for a form of knowledge as indubitable as mathematical truth — anticipates Descartes by five centuries.',
